 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S445-007\",\n \"clauses\": {\"explore_pattern_across_cases\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F08\", \"template\": \"Cover each {shape} with unit square tiles and count them: {shown}. Which rule always gives the number of tiles?\", \"reasoning\": \"F08 asks the learner to cover each shape with tiles and count across cases, then asks which rule always gives the count. This is exploration across several cases.\"},\n  \"reason_inductively_from_cases\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F09\", \"template\": \"Three {shape}s are tiled with unit squares: {shown}. Which rule works for every one of them?\", \"reasoning\": \"F09 shows three cases and asks for one rule that works for every case, which is reasoning inductively from cases. The word inductively is in introduces_vocab, so it may be used here.\"},\n  \"derive_area_formula\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F02\", \"template\": \"Each {area_label} is the {dims_word} multiplied together.\", \"reasoning\": \"F02 is the derivation hint for the derive_formula task type. It states that the area is the dimensions multiplied together.\"},\n  \"area_formula_expression\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F04\", \"template\": \"So the rule is: {area_label} = {answer}.\", \"reasoning\": \"F04 prints the area formula as an expression, with the answer domain giving the rectangle and square forms.\"},\n  \"area_attribute\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F02\", \"template\": \"Each {area_label} is the {dims_word} multiplied together.\", \"reasoning\": \"The area_label slot is nested from F01, which prints area, a word in cumulative_vocab. F02 names the area of each figure.\"},\n  \"square_figure\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F08\", \"template\": \"Cover each {shape} with unit square tiles and count them: {shown}. Which rule always gives the number of tiles?\", \"reasoning\": \"The shape slot of F08 prints square, so F08 produces problems about a square.\"},\n  \"rectangle_figure\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F08\", \"template\": \"Cover each {shape} with unit square tiles and count them: {shown}. Which rule always gives the number of tiles?\", \"reasoning\": \"The shape slot of F08 prints rectangle, so F08 produces problems about a rectangle.\"},\n  \"square_tile_array\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F08\", \"template\": \"Cover each {shape} with unit square tiles and count them: {shown}. Which rule always gives the number of tiles?\", \"reasoning\": \"F08 covers each figure with unit square tiles, which is a tile-array representation.\"},\n  \"square_tile_unit\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F08\", \"template\": \"Cover each {shape} with unit square tiles and count them: {shown}. Which rule always gives the number of tiles?\", \"reasoning\": \"F08 names the unit square tiles that measure each figure. The words tile and unit appear in the competency text.\"}},\n \"failing_families\": [\"F02\", \"F03\", \"F05\", \"F08\", \"F09\", \"F10\", \"F11\"],\n \"failing_templates\": [\"Each {area_label} is the {dims_word} multiplied together.\", \"Look at each {shape_noun}: {worked}.\", \"{x} × {y} = {z}\", \"Cover each {shape} with unit square tiles and count them: {shown}. Which rule always gives the number of tiles?\", \"Three {shape}s are tiled with unit squares: {shown}. Which rule works for every one of them?\", \"{article} {r} by {c} {shape} takes {t} tiles\", \"{head}, and {case}\"],\n \"overall\": \"FAIL\",\n \"reasoning\": \"Four faults. (1) F05 draws x, y and z from three separate domains with no pairing, and F10 draws r, c and t the same way, and its shape slot is independent of r and c. So the domains allow a 10 by 10 square-or-rectangle with 4 tiles, and a square with unequal sides, to print. F03 prints F05 and F08, F09 and F11 print F10, so they carry the same false statements. (2) F02 with dims_word side prints a sentence that says the area is the side multiplied together, which is misleading for a square. (3) sentence_max_words is 22. F08's own fixed words are 10 before the shown text, and F11 always prints two F10 items of 8 words plus 'and' (17 words), so F08 is always 27 words in one sentence, counting numerals as words. F09 is 7 fixed words plus 17, so 24 words in every render. F11 and F10 print that text too. (4) Not failed on this alone: the word width appears in no cumulative_vocab or introduces_vocab entry and not in the competency text, and it is used by F02, F04, F06 and F07. The builder should decide whether width is in scope for this lc. The words tile, unit, cover and rule are either in the competency text or generic English.\"}",
